      "content": "Yes — and notice what you just did: you caught your own error before I said anything. That is exactly the exam skill that earns marks. 💪\n\nThe fields point towards S (up the y-axis) and towards T (along the x-axis), so they are **perpendicular**. Perpendicular vectors are never added directly — they combine with Pythagoras.\n\n$$E_{\\text{net}} = \\sqrt{E_S^2 + E_T^2} = \\sqrt{2700^2 + 300^2} = \\sqrt{7\\,380\\,000} = 2\\,716{,}62\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{C}^{-1}$$\n\n**Final Answer:** $E_{\\text{net}} = 2{,}72 \\times 10^3\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{C}^{-1}$ (accept $2\\,716{,}62\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{C}^{-1}$)\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $E = \\dfrac{kQ}{r^2}$ for each charge, then $E_{\\text{net}} = \\sqrt{E_S^2 + E_T^2}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $E$: the strength of the electric field at that point, measured in $\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{C}^{-1}$ (how hard the field would push or pull on 1 coulomb of charge)\n  * $k$: Coulomb's constant, $9 \\times 10^9\\ \\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}^2\\cdot\\text{C}^{-2}$ — the fixed strength of the electrical force in air/vacuum\n  * $Q$: the size of the charge creating the field, in coulombs (here $3 \\times 10^{-9}\\text{ C}$ — use the size only, the minus sign just tells you the direction)\n  * $r$: the distance from that charge to the point where you want the field, in metres ($0{,}1\\text{ m}$ for S, $0{,}3\\text{ m}$ for T)\n* **Why we use it:** Each charge creates its own field at the origin, and fields are vectors — they have size *and* direction. Because S sits on the y-axis and T on the x-axis, their two fields meet at $90^\\circ$, so we combine them like the sides of a right-angled triangle instead of simply adding them.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** The mark that learners lose most often here is the **direction** mark — always state which way each field points (towards a negative charge, away from a positive one) *before* combining. And never write $E_{\\text{net}} = E_S + E_T$ unless the charges are on the same straight line. If the question asks for direction too, the net field here points at $\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\tfrac{2700}{300}\\right) \\approx 83{,}7^\\circ$ above the positive x-axis."
