      "content": "Yes — the $1\\text{ V}$ is lost *inside* the battery, so the terminal potential difference is smaller than the emf.\n\n$$V_{\\text{ext}} = \\varepsilon - Ir = 12 - (2)(0{,}5) = 12 - 1 = 11\\text{ V}$$\n\n**Final Answer:** The voltmeter reads $11\\text{ V}$.\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\varepsilon = V_{\\text{ext}} + Ir$  (rearranged: $V_{\\text{ext}} = \\varepsilon - Ir$)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $\\varepsilon$: the emf — the total energy the battery gives to each coulomb of charge (in volts, $\\text{V}$)\n  * $V_{\\text{ext}}$: the terminal potential difference — the energy per coulomb that actually reaches the external circuit, which is exactly what the voltmeter across the battery terminals reads\n  * $I$: the current flowing through the whole circuit (in amperes, $\\text{A}$)\n  * $r$: the internal resistance — the small resistance inside the battery itself (in ohms, $\\Omega$)\n* **Why we use it:** A real battery is not perfect. Some energy is always \"lost\" pushing charge through its own internal resistance ($Ir$). The voltmeter is connected across the battery's terminals, so it measures what is *left over* after that loss — never the full emf while current is flowing.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** The voltmeter across a battery reads $V_{\\text{ext}} = \\varepsilon - Ir$, **not** $\\varepsilon + Ir$. A very common mistake is adding the lost volts instead of subtracting them — remember, the terminal voltage can only be *less* than the emf when current flows. Also keep the unit as $\\text{V}$ (not $\\text{A}$ or $\\Omega$) for the final mark, and note that if the switch were opened (no current), the voltmeter would read the full $12\\text{ V}$."
